AB  - Large language models have shown promising reasoning and planning abilities, but their direct use in real-time strategy decision making remains limited by partial observability, delayed action effects, and numerical inconsistency. In StarCraft II, a candidate action may be syntactically valid at the current moment yet lead to resource starvation, production blockage, or unfavorable combat losses a few seconds later. This paper proposes NovaWM, an action-conditioned world model for short-horizon state simulation in StarCraft II. NovaWM represents game observations as structured text, augments them with a Historical Memory module for tracking previously visible enemy entities under the fog of war, and performs prompt-guided hierarchical state transition reasoning before producing the future observation. The transition process is decomposed into economic calculation, queue progression, spatial movement, and combat attrition, which reduces the burden of end-to-end generation. Based on SC2-Dynamics-50k, we construct SC2-Dynamics-50k-HM with historical memory and fine-tune the model using low-rank adaptation. Experiments show that NovaWM improves prediction accuracy for economy, queue progress, hit points, and enemy spatial consistency over zero-shot large language models and an end-to-end world-model baseline. Downstream closed-loop evaluation provides preliminary evidence that world-model feedback can improve action refinement in high-difficulty games.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
