AB  - We propose an island-based network generation algorithm that simultaneously produces the three fundamental characteristics of complex networks: scale-free, small-world, and community structures. Our model, the Island-based Scale-Free Small-World (IsSFSW) model, preliminarily generates multiple highly clustered scale-free communities (islands) through preferential attachment with full emphasis on triad formation, and subsequently applies global link rewiring to achieve the small-world effects. Through systematic analysis of geometric properties of networks across varying input parameters, we confirm that the IsSFSW model exhibits the above three fundamental characteristics at the same time. Moreover, the IsSFSW model can control the characteristics between the scale-free side and small-world side by adjusting the number of islands parameter to some extent and can even possess a truncation of power-law in degree distributions. We investigate the dynamics of disease propagation and spatial prisoner's dilemma games using multi-agent simulations as typical examples to understand how dynamics appear on this network model. The IsSFSW model has the potential to generate networks with higher reproducibility when the target network is clearly defined, owing to its algorithmic flexibility and simplicity, and simultaneous manifestation of network characteristics. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
